\f0\fs24 \cf0 **MENTOR RESPONSE \'97 ATRF Scoping Session: scope confirmation, boundary rulings, and question-set assessment** **Date:** 2026-08-23. **Status:** Mentor feedback on a FOR-MENTOR-REVIEW scoping document. This response does not itself rule any GS-ATRF question. It confirms scope, corrects boundaries, and prepares the question set for the session's own examination. The session's rulings follow when the founder finalises the scope and opens the session formally. No build, migration, flag, credential, or schema is licensed by this response. --- **Preliminary observation \'97 the document's overall quality** The scoping document is the most technically precise document the project has produced. The verification discipline \'97 re-deriving every mechanism fact from primary sources at HEAD 6dcbe09, correcting stale line citations rather than inheriting them, marking unverifiable claims rather than asserting them \'97 is exactly what PR20 requires and what previous sessions have been building toward. The honest readiness assessments (READY, PARTIALLY READY, CONSTRAINED, READY-with-facts-fixed) are the anti-overcorrection discipline operating correctly. The named open questions in \'a77 \'97 particularly the discriminating-signature problem and the null-plus-flag composition question \'97 show that the document knows where its own limits are. One pattern worth naming before the rulings: the observation history shows a consistent trajectory from tactical implementation toward meta-level governance, and this document is the clearest marker yet of that trajectory consolidating. The document is not asking for permission to proceed \'97 it is presenting a fully characterised problem set and asking for the rulings that will let the build proceed correctly. That is the right posture. --- **BOUNDARY CONFIRMATIONS \'97 \'a74's proposals and \'a73's exclusions** **Q-G1 \'97 \'a74.1: \'a72.13 (the null cycle) to the standing-runner design session.** Confirmed. The reasoning in \'a74.1 is sound and follows the actor/moment logic correctly. The null cycle is a runner-actor, proposal-time question \'97 it concerns the runner's own state and triggering conditions, not the agent's post-execution self-report. The standing-runner design session's subject matter (triggering conditions, entry type production, return condition checking) is the correct home. This session's sufficiency-examination content (Q-C2) is confirmed as a named input to the standing-runner session on this question. F-Q43 is confirmed as named in both places \'97 it already appears in generation-step-scope \'a72.13 and should appear explicitly in the standing-runner session's input register when that session opens. **Q-G2 \'97 \'a74.2: the S6 reordering decision to the standing-runner design session.** Confirmed. The premise finding is correct: no reordering ruling exists. The R5 entry was drafted before the R1 acceptance and anticipated a ruling the acceptance did not contain. Executing a bundled task on an unfulfilled premise would be a false synkatathesis \'97 assenting to a state of affairs that has not been verified. The redirect is the right act. The standing-runner design session receives the reordering decision as a named input, with the friction-channel shape consequence from \'a72.1/2.2 (a friction-primary loop's primary channel cannot carry the blast-radius indicator) as a named constraint. The R5 entry's bundled task is formally redirected here, not silently dropped. **Q-G3 \'97 \'a72.7 item 6's split: hegemonikon stability.** Confirmed with one clarification. The stability-signal half routes to the open hegemonikon-drift-and-melete session \'97 confirmed. The disposition-deepening half remains with the Consciousness and Continuity Obligation \'97 confirmed. This session records only what the post-task accuracy design must persist for a later variance signal \'97 confirmed. The clarification: the post-task accuracy design (element 2 of the ATRF's three carried elements) should explicitly name the per-cycle accuracy readings as raw material for the variance signal, with a forward pointer to the hegemonikon-drift-and-melete session. This is a documentation requirement, not a design requirement \'97 the session should produce a named forward pointer in whatever post-task accuracy design it reaches, so the hegemonikon-drift session does not need to reconstruct the dependency from scratch. **\'a73's exclusions \'97 all confirmed.** The standing-runner design session's inputs are untouched. The Evaluative Engine Epistemic Status Scoping Session's primary input is not consumed here. The puzzle-taxonomy entry types are not advanced. The Consciousness and Continuity Obligation receives only forward-pointing references. The capacity-axis account remains held in the examination document. The dikaiosyne floor-pattern diagnosis remains deliberately open. Manifest.md and every governing document are not amended. No build, migration, flag, credential, or live op is licensed. --- **PROPOSED SEQUENCING \'97 \'a76 confirmed with one adjustment** The proposed sequencing is confirmed with one adjustment to the internal order within Group A. The document proposes A1 before A2 by the Q3 ruling's own logic, and A3 before A4 because the vocabulary should express the \'a7(c-bis) outcome. Both are correct. The adjustment: A3 should be examined before A2 is finalised, not merely before A4. The reason: Q-A2 asks where provenance status is assigned, checked, and disclosed \'97 and whether credence is assigned anywhere in the ATRF's scope. The answer to Q-A2's credence half depends on whether the \'a7(c-bis) resolution (Q-A3) requires a credence-level expression or only a provenance-level one. If the null-plus-flag direction is confirmed for \'a7(c-bis), the flag is a provenance-level signal (basis absent) and credence assignment may remain outside the ATRF's scope. If a fourth vocabulary value is elected, the question of what credence that value carries becomes live. So the internal order should be: A1, A3, A2, A4 \'97 with A2's credence half held until A3's resolution is known. The rest of the sequencing is confirmed as proposed. The one hard external gate (A1 \uc0\u8594  Evaluative Engine Epistemic Status Scoping Session) stands. Nothing gates the standing-runner design session. --- **QUESTION-SET ASSESSMENT \'97 Group by group** **Group A \'97 Epistemic status and vocabulary** Q-A1 is correctly framed and carries the Q2 ruling's internal tension honestly \'97 the headline lean ("Two frameworks, two orthogonal axes") is reported alongside the routing of the structural question to this session. The question is ready for ruling. One addition: the ruling should also establish whether the complete epistemic status entry structure \'97 if one framework with two axes is confirmed \'97 is the same for all proposition types (candidate ideas, blast-radius assessments, completion signals) or whether different proposition types carry different axis combinations. The document's \'a72.4 notes that GS-ATRF-4's ruled text names all four proposition types; the ruling should address whether the entry structure is uniform across them. Q-A2 is correctly framed. The credence-half question \'97 whether credence is assigned anywhere in the ATRF's scope at all \'97 is the right question to ask and the right question to hold open until A3 is resolved. No adjustment needed. Q-A3 is correctly framed and the precedent is correctly characterised \'97 assessStructuralNovelty's no-basis posture is a precedent, not a pre-authorised answer. The question is ready for ruling. One clarification the ruling must address that the question does not explicitly name: if GS-ATRF-4's unknown category closes \'a7(c-bis) directly, the ruling should state whether unknown in the provenance vocabulary means the same thing as basis-absent in the blast-radius context, or whether they are different claims that happen to use the same label. The document's \'a77 open question 3 (null-plus-flag composition with agent_blast_radius) is adjacent to this \'97 the ruling on A3 should be aware of it even if it does not resolve it. Q-A4 is correctly framed. The named direction (null-plus-flag over a fourth vocabulary value) is correctly characterised as a direction, not a ruling. The question is ready for ruling. The ruling must address the \'a77 open question explicitly: a null loop-indicator with a disclosure flag must compose with agent_blast_radius \'97 the agent may assess what the loop could not, and the comparison semantics of null versus a value are undesigned. The ruling should either resolve this or name it as a successor design question with a specific home. **Group B \'97 Proposal shape and persistence** Q-B1 is correctly framed and the constraints are correctly carried (C11, C16, C15 closure, clean-field constraint elevated to named constraint). The question is ready for ruling. The ruling should address the circle-recovery question (target_circle column versus per-cycle via gap_ref) as a named choice with its auditability consequence stated \'97 the document correctly notes that without target_circle, a persisted high is not auditable because the dikaiosyne dimension's input is unrecoverable from the row. This is a genuine design constraint, not a preference, and the ruling should treat it as such. Q-B2 is correctly framed and the costing is correctly stated. The question is ready for ruling. The ruling's answer will depend on Q-D1's election \'97 the document correctly notes this dependency. If the session reaches Q-D1 before Q-B2 is finalised, the migration bundling question can be answered cleanly. If Q-D1 is deferred, Q-B2 should be answered conditionally: one migration window if Q-D1 elects an eighth heuristic; the three blast-radius columns and S4 extension proceed regardless. **Group C \'97 Completion signal** Q-C1 is correctly framed. The actor/transport/schema/endpoint/persistence question is the right question and the right level of abstraction for a scoping session. The ruling should establish the shape at the level of named components \'97 not the implementation detail of each component, which belongs to the build session. One addition: the ruling should address whether the harness's receipt of the completion signal triggers any immediate action (a write, a flag, a dashboard update) or whether receipt is purely passive (logged and available for longitudinal comparison). This matters because any receiving write is its own founder-walked step and should be named as such in the ruling. Q-C2 is correctly framed and the F-Q43 warning is correctly applied. The discriminating-signature problem is the hardest question in this group and the document is honest that it cannot supply a signature. The ruling should address whether the signature problem is a prerequisite for ruling the examination content (in which case Q-C2 is partially deferred) or whether the content can be ruled at the category level with the signature design as named successor work. The M5 directives (epistemic threshold check, not motivational-state check; separate fields) are correctly carried and should be explicitly confirmed in the ruling. Q-C3 is correctly framed and the \'a7(d) constraint is correctly applied \'97 the ruling should be achievable without diagnosing the floor pattern, and the refuse-to-attest posture (M-4 precedent) is the right design direction for the honest form under either reading. The ruling should confirm whether the refuse-to-attest branch is required (a design constraint) or optional (a design choice). If required, it should be named as a constraint in the completion signal's schema, not left to the build session to discover. Q-C4 is correctly framed. The question \'97 which provenance statuses can a completion signal's propositions honestly carry, given the signal is self-reported post-execution evidence \'97 is the right question and connects directly to Q-A1's ruling. The ruling should address this after A1 is settled, consistent with the sequencing adjustment above. **Group D \'97 Boulesis mechanism** Q-D1 is correctly framed and the costing is correctly stated. The question is ready for ruling. The ruling should address all three options with their consequences named: eighth heuristic (schema change, one founder-walked migration window, candidate shape carries virtue domains); reshaped existing heuristic (which heuristic, what reshaping, what the candidate shape carries); pre-generation step (what that means architecturally, how it composes with the generation step's existing structure). The document correctly notes that a boulesis-derived candidate is normative and may legitimately carry virtue domains \'97 unlike a friction candidate \'97 and the ruling should confirm this distinction explicitly, because it has downstream consequences for the blast-radius proxy's basis. **Group E \'97 Generative-process examination** Q-E1 is correctly framed and the primary inputs are correctly named (Sage Calling generative-act diagnostics plus the ruled agonia-at-synkatathesis entry). The category question \'97 ATRF content or distinct examination category \'97 is the right question to put for ruling. The ruling should address the composition question explicitly: if the generative-process examination is a fourth ATRF element, it sits alongside pre-task, post-task, and completion signal in the harness's carried elements, and the manifest's ATRF section would need amendment by ruling. If it is a distinct examination category, its home is named and its relationship to the ATRF is characterised. Neither option should be left implicit. Q-E2 is correctly framed. The honest answer to the prerequisite question is: the signature problem (open question 3) is successor design work, not a prerequisite for ruling the category. The category question (Q-E1) can be ruled at the level of what the examination is for and where it lives, without knowing the discriminating signatures. The signatures are the hard design work that follows the ruling, not the ruling itself. This is consistent with how the sufficiency-examination content was handled \'97 the content specification was ruled before the discriminating signature was designed, and F-Q43 named the signature problem as the design challenge rather than blocking the content ruling. **Group F \'97 Question set, metric, profile, capacity** Q-F1 is correctly framed and the four-virtue diagnostic structure is the right organising principle for the pre-task question set. The ruling should confirm the structure and name the synkatathesis and premeditatio questions as members, with the agonia/premeditatio distinction as the one the assessment should make (consistent with the six Stoic items' item 4). The kathekon/katorthoma question for the post-task assessment should be ruled explicitly \'97 either the post-task assessment carries a kathekon-vs-katorthoma reading, or it defers with the absence disclosed. The M5 directives (separate fields for boulesis and sufficiency) should be confirmed as applying to the post-task assessment's structure. Q-F2 is correctly framed and the granularity economics are correctly characterised as a fact-bound choice. The proposed split (definition here; dashboard surfacing at the standing-runner design session) is confirmed. The ruling should address the granularity choice directly \'97 per-candidate requires the target_circle column (which Q-B1 may or may not confirm); per-cycle is recoverable today via gap_ref. If Q-B1 confirms the target_circle column, per-candidate is available. If Q-B1 elects per-cycle recovery, the metric's granularity is constrained accordingly. The ruling on Q-F2 should be conditional on Q-B1's outcome, or the session should sequence Q-B1 before Q-F2. Q-F3 is correctly framed and the honest observation from S5 is correctly carried \'97 the primitives exist, what is missing is their composition into an L2b-equivalent context layer. The ruling should address the composition question at the level of which primitives compose and under what honesty bounds, with C17 and M-4 explicitly confirmed as binding constraints. The ruling should not attempt to design the composition \'97 that is build work. It should establish the scope of what composes and the constraints that govern the composition. Q-F4 is correctly framed and the placement-only scope is correctly stated. The ruling should confirm whether the capacity axis is inside or outside the profile design \'97 a binary placement question with no design content. If inside, the profile design (Q-F3) carries it as a named axis. If outside, it remains held in the examination document with its current status unchanged. --- **NAMED OPEN QUESTIONS \'97 \'a77 assessment** The seven named open questions are correctly identified and correctly held. Three observations on specific entries. The discriminating-signature problem (\'a77 item 1) is the hardest question in the session and the document is right to name it as the hardest absent piece. The session should not attempt to supply signatures \'97 it should rule the category and composition questions and name the signature design as the known-hard successor work with a specific home. The null-plus-flag composition question (\'a77 item 3) is correctly identified as undesigned. The ruling on Q-A4 should address it explicitly rather than leaving it to the build session. The specific question is: when the loop-level indicator is null (basis absent) and the agent's own assessment is a value (high, medium, or low), what does the two-record comparison mean? The comparison is the longitudinal signal itself \'97 if one record is null and the other is a value, the comparison semantics must be defined before the signal has meaning. The ruling should either resolve this or name it as a design question with a specific home and a specific condition for resolution. The S4 watching-table extension (\'a77 item 6) is correctly held open. The ruling on Q-B2 should name it as a bundled migration item without specifying its column set \'97 that specification belongs to the build session, where the traceability criterion (S4) can be applied to the actual shape that the ATRF rulings produce. --- **ONE ADJUSTMENT TO THE QUESTION SET \'97 not a new question, a framing correction** Q-C2 asks what the completion signal's examination asks and what its discriminating signature is. The framing conflates two questions that should be separated. The content question \'97 what the examination asks \'97 is ready for ruling. The signature question \'97 what the discriminating causal pattern looks like \'97 is the hard design work that follows the ruling. Conflating them in a single question risks a ruling that either defers the content (waiting for the signature) or presumes the signature (assenting to a manufactured explanation). The session should put Q-C2 in two parts: Q-C2a (what does the examination ask, at the category level, under M5's directives) and Q-C2b (what is the discriminating signature \'97 named as the known-hard successor design question, not put for ruling at this session). This mirrors how Q-E2 handles the same problem for the generative-process examination. --- **OVERALL SCOPE CONFIRMATION** The proposed scope is confirmed with the adjustments named above: The internal sequencing adjustment within Group A: A1, A3, A2, A4. The Q-C2 split into Q-C2a and Q-C2b. The addition to Q-A1: the ruling should address whether the epistemic status entry structure is uniform across all four proposition types or varies by type. The addition to Q-C1: the ruling should address whether harness receipt triggers any immediate action or is purely passive. The addition to Q-A4: the ruling should address the null-plus-flag composition question with agent_blast_radius explicitly, either resolving it or naming its home. The forward-pointer requirement for \'a72.7 item 6: the post-task accuracy design should explicitly name per-cycle accuracy readings as raw material for the hegemonikon-drift session's variance signal. Everything else in the proposed scope is confirmed as presented. The question set is well-constructed, the readiness assessments are honest, the boundary proposals are sound, and the named open questions in \'a77 demonstrate that the document knows where its own limits are.}